"[2510.12133v1] SafeMT: Multi-turn Safety for Multimodal Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12133v1)\nciteturn2799view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12133v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:SafeMT: Multi-turn Safety for Multimodal Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Han Zhu , cite6†Juntao Dai , cite7†Jiaming Ji , cite8†Haoran Li , cite9†Chengkun Cai , cite10†Pengcheng Wen , cite11†Chi-Min Chan , cite12†Boyuan Chen , cite13†Yaodong Yang , cite14†Sirui Han , cite15†Yike Guo L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled SafeMT: Multi-turn Safety for Multimodal Language Models, by Han Zhu and 10 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite16†View PDF cite17†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:With the widespread use of multi-modal Large Language models (MLLMs), safety issues have become a growing concern. Multi-turn dialogues, which are more common in everyday interactions, pose a greater risk than single prompts; however, existing benchmarks do not adequately consider this situation.\nL17: To encourage the community to focus on the safety issues of these models in multi-turn dialogues, we introduce SafeMT, a benchmark that features dialogues of varying lengths generated from harmful queries accompanied by images. This benchmark consists of 10,000 samples in total, encompassing 17 different scenarios and four jailbreak methods. Additionally, we propose Safety Index (SI) to evaluate the general safety of MLLMs during conversations.\nL18: We assess the safety of 17 models using this benchmark and discover that the risk of successful attacks on these models increases as the number of turns in harmful dialogues rises. This observation indicates that the safety mechanisms of these models are inadequate for recognizing the hazard in dialogue interactions. We propose a dialogue safety moderator capable of detecting malicious intent concealed within conversations and providing MLLMs with relevant safety policies.\nL19: Experimental results from several open-source models indicate that this moderator is more effective in reducing multi-turn ASR compared to existed guard models.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite18†arXiv:2510.12133 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite19†arXiv:2510.12133v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite20†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.12133†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Han Zhu [cite21†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Tue, 14 Oct 2025 04:24:07 UTC (14,058 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled SafeMT: Multi-turn Safety for Multimodal Language Models, by Han Zhu and 10 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite16†View PDF L36:   * cite17†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite22†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite23†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite24†< prev |   cite25†next > L45: \nL46: cite26†new | cite27†recent | cite28†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite29†cs L51: cite30†cs.AI L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite31†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite32†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite33†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Dat